{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And you shall place the blessing upon Mount Gerizim</b> — as its Targum has it: יָת מְבָרְכַיָּא, that is, אֶת הַמְבָרְכִים (those who bless), upon Mount Gerizim, meaning toward Mount Gerizim; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 11:29). But this is not correct according to the plain sense, for He has not yet given any commandment regarding those who bless. Rather, its meaning is: I am placing before you a way of blessing and a way of curse, and the sense of <b>before you</b> is that you shall choose for yourselves from them what you desire, and I declare that the blessing shall be yours when you perform the commandments, and the curse if you do not uphold them. This is as He says further (Deuteronomy 30:15): See, I have set before you this day life and good, and death and evil, etc. And afterwards He says here that they shall place this blessing, which He is declaring to them, upon Mount Gerizim, and the curse upon Mount Ebal — and behold, it is given orally, as in וְנָתַן אֹתָם עַל רֹאשׁ הַשָּׂעִיר (and he shall place them upon the head of the scapegoat) (Leviticus 16:21), according to the words of R. Abraham ibn Ezra (ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 11:29). Now here He speaks by way of admonition, that they shall place the blessing upon Mount Gerizim, to bless the people if they heed the commandments of HaShem, and the curse upon Mount Ebal, saying: Cursed be he who does not heed the commandments and turns aside from the way that HaShem has commanded. And it would be possible that all the tribes pronounce the blessing and all of them the curse, or that the Levites say all of it, or that one individual proclaims it to all of them; and afterwards He returns to explain it there (below, 27:12–14): And these shall stand to bless the people upon Mount Gerizim, and these shall stand for the curse, and He says: And the Levites shall answer, and He spells out the entire matter — for here, because of the admonition He expressed when He said See, I am placing, etc., He mentioned part of the commandment and returned to explain it in its proper place. And it is possible that Mount Gerizim was to the south, which is the right side, and Mount Ebal to the north, as it is written (Jeremiah 1:14): From the north the evil shall break forth."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:48:18.703597907Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}